          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"eliot-middlemarch-5b8f9f1880f46aef\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Lydgate directly avows his love for Laure, giving the utterance strong truth-conditional force. In its immediate placement within a marriage proposal, it also helps enact a serious relational undertaking, though the explicit commitment is carried more strongly by the surrounding requests and promise to wait. His passion and impetuosity do not establish expressive/reflexive force: the declaration is deliberate and forms part of a resolved suit.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The degree of performative force is somewhat uncertain because “I love you” supports the proposal, while the adjacent clauses more explicitly express the proposed commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lydgate directly addresses Laure during a deliberate declaration of love and proposal of marriage.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Lydgate is intensely infatuated and recognizes his conduct as resembling a sudden mad impulse, yet he has deliberately sought Laure out with the resolved intention of declaring adoration and asking her to marry him. The utterance is therefore principally an avowal integrated into courtship and a proposed marital undertaking, not a reflex-like verbal discharge. Laure’s subsequent revelation exposes his idealization but does not alter the utterance’s core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“You are lonely; I love you; I want you to consent to be my wife”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit declaration presents Lydgate’s love as true and is immediately integrated into a marriage proposal.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I will wait, but I want you to promise that you will marry me—no one else.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding turn supplies moderate performative support by framing the declaration as part of a proposed exclusive marital undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Before visiting, Lydgate is described as “bent on telling her that he adored her, and on asking her to marry him” and as resolved to do it.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration is planned and purposive rather than positively presented as an involuntary expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator calls the episode an example of Lydgate’s “fitful swerving of passion” and says he knew his conduct resembled “the sudden impulse of a madman.”\",\"supports\":\"The context establishes intense, impetuous passion, but not independent expressive/reflexive force in the core words.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while P captures its secondary role in an enacted marriage suit. Intensity and impetuosity remain contextual features, so neither E nor O is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
